WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.280
Getting a robot to fly is impressive

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.820
but once it’s in the air, how do you keep it there

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.930
without draining too much energy?

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.100
Nature’s answer is perching.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.980
Think of perching as landing without a runway.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.000
Birds, bats, &amp; insects do it.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:15.500
 

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.800
They can land on narrow surfaces, upside down

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.660
or right-side up.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.520
Remain idle for long periods of time,

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.340
and then take off by launching themselves

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.980
from different positions.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.880
Now researchers have made a robotic insect

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.760
that can perch by using electroadhesion.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.900
The tiny robot is adorned with an “electrode patch”

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.580
that can create a static charge for attaching to surfaces.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.060
It’s a similar principle to static cling from a balloon.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.520
This type of adhesion is pretty weak, so objects

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.379
must be really light for them to stick to a surface.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:51.859
To get around that limitation, scientists designed

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.460
these robots to weigh a minuscule 84mg.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.980
That’s less than a bee! At this weight, It takes

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.260
a thousand times less power to perch than to fly.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.480
The lightweight electrode patch combined

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:07.160
with a flexible, shock absorbing mount

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.380
enables the robot to perch on a variety of surfaces:

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.500
glass, wood, and even a leaf.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.720
To detach, the electricity is turned off

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.800
allowing the patch to discharge.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.580
Once they can fly tetherless, these perching robots could

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.980
be used to provide birds-eye views of disaster areas,

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.140
detect hazardous chemicals,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.260
or enable communication in remote regions.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.290
Future designs could allow for perching
on vertical surfaces,

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:38.890
which means we could have our first robotic

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.150
fly on the wall.

